------------------

there is some profoundly troubling code in here that clearly was
put there to optimize the code that whitesmith's was emitting:

declaring scalar types static when the compiler runs out of registers
will result in faster, smaller and very very broken code if the function
being called could have more than one process running in it due to sleeping
on a disk i/o.

these 2 processes will step on each other horribly.

ANY static declarations in any functions are very suspect, and either
need a lock, with all the deadlock possiblities that implies, or we
need to just KNOW that it, and any functions it calls, cannot ever sleep.


tracking this down is simply a matter of searching for the string 'static'.

--------------------

The u struct lives in its own 4K segment (USERSEG), remapped per
process, and the rest of that page is padding.  Pack that padding with
pure-text leaf functions - the ccc runtime (csv / q* / ldiv / tramp) and
whatever other dataless helpers fit - so the page is not wasted, and
move as much of it in there as possible.

Identify and move constant data onto the page too: the static strings
(the devname / panic messages), the syscall dispatch table (syssw), the
block and char switch tables (biosw / ciosw), the specs / mws drive
tables.  They are identical across processes and read but never written,
so they belong on the filled-out page and fork skips copying them the
same way.  Audit each one for writes first - anything ever written has
to stay in ordinary data.

Then on fork, rather than segcopy-ing the whole page (the u struct plus
the shared leaf code) into a fresh segment each time, keep a freelist
of "filled out" pages that already carry the leaf code.  fork takes one
of those, copies only the ~629 byte u struct into it, and skips the text
copy; exit returns the page to the list instead of freeing it.  The leaf
code is identical across every process, so this saves both the copy and
the re-fill on every fork.

Open: where the list lives, how a fresh page is primed the first time
(one segcopy of the leaf code), and how a swapped-out process interacts
with it.

The u page is laid out by the linker as three objects in one segment
(USERSEG, sys.h): the leaf code first at the base, then ustack (user.c)
- the system stack - and then the u struct itself, whose register save
area comes first in the struct because that is where the firmware's
trap entry leaves SP.  So the kernel's C stack grows down from
&u.stack into ustack.

ustack and u are .bss, and the file carries no bytes for either.  That
is what let the page move up to segment 15: the kernel and the loader
never run at the same time, so the loader's stack lands on the u
struct's addresses during the load, and the page only has to keep its
*code* below the loader.  task0_body() zeroes u before it is used,
since what the loader leaves there is its own stack.

--------------------

Three window pages used to have to exist at once: 0xd000 for the
superblock, 0xe000 for the u page, 0xf000 for the buffer data window.
The same three pages are in play, but the u page has moved up into 15
(0xf000) and the buffer window took its place at 0xe000, so what is
held now is 0xe000 by the window and 0xd000 only for the length of a
user-memory copy (mem.s borrows and restores it).  It matters because
the buffer pool's headers are minted contiguously off &blist[8] ==
_ebss, so a page held by anything else hides whatever headers land in
it - 0xd000's page was dead space for exactly that reason.

The superblock used to hold 0xd000 across calls and sleeps, which meant
that page could not carry headers - the pool stopped at 0xd000.  It is
plain memory now: getsb() (uio.c) copies the block into a per-device
sbslot in .bss once and points the buffer's data/xmem at the slot, so
sb->data stays an ordinary pointer, nothing has to be re-pinned after a
sleep, and the driver still writes the right bytes because its DMA
address is (xmem << 12) | (data & 0xfff), not the window address.  mem.s
restores the page it borrows for user memory from IMAGE0+26, so with the
superblock out of the window system nothing holds 0xd000 and the headers
carry on through it.  bzero() and bget()'s reuse path call sbrel() to
give a recycled buffer its window mapping back.

The cost is the slots: NMOUNT * (512 + 6) = 2072 bytes of .bss, about 98
headers, so the pool gains ~90 rather than the ~195 the page is worth.
The two leftovers are still available if the pool ever needs to be
bigger again:

  - Put the superblock back in a segment and borrow window 13 for each
    access (save the mapping, map, copy, restore), with bcopy() moved
    into the u page so only u-page code runs while the borrow is held.
    That gives up the ~2072 bytes of slots and frees the page for
    headers - the full ~195 - but requires a discipline the slot design
    does not: no header access (bget/brelse/bdwrite) between mapping the
    window and restoring it.  A miss there is silent filesystem
    corruption, which is why the slots went in first.

  - The u page is in segment 15 now, so the pool's ceiling is one page
    away (0xf000 instead of the window's 0xe000) and it roughly
    doubles.  It needs the buffer data window to become borrowed at
    every site that uses it: bwin() holds its segment for the whole of
    a buffer access, so headers parked in 0xe000's page would be read
    back as buffer data.  Borrowing means save the map register, map,
    copy, restore - and no header access (bget/brelse/bdwrite) while a
    borrow is held, the same discipline the superblock slots avoided.
    expand_bufs()'s bound is BUFWIN (sys.h) today; this is the change
    that moves it up to _upage.

The boot loader's own stack sits at the very top of memory (mwboot1.s
parks sp at 0xffff and the second level inherits it), and the loader
keeps using it for the whole kernel load.  Measured in d1 by sampling sp
per instruction over the whole trap-free pre-kernel window (cycle 462452
to 709169), the loader's deepest sp is 0xffde - thirty-three bytes below
the top, so the loader's stack is not what caps the image.  What caps it
is the block rounding: boot.c's load() writes whole 512 byte blocks from
0x0ff0, so the last block starts at 0xfdf0, and the 128-block list
reaches past the top of memory before it runs out - the practical ceiling
is 120 blocks.

The kernel is linked so the file's data stream stops short of the
loader's stack: 0x1000 + text + data, which today is 0xf000 + the leaf
code's size, ends at 0xfcc9 - 119 blocks, 789 bytes clear of the
loader's deepest sp.  What actually binds the leaf code now is the u
page itself, not the loader: leaf + ustack (512) + u (117) has to fit
in 4096, so the leaf code has 0xf3e bytes and is using 0xcc9, leaving
194 bytes to grow into.

The same sampling gives the kernel's own stack: the deepest supervisor
sp is 0xede5 at adiv+21, 228 bytes of the 512 byte ustack, so the
number in user.c is generous and is the one to turn if the page ever
has to get smaller.

--------------------

V7's big exec change over V6 was the environment: exec(2) takes a third
argument, an array of "NAME=value" strings (envp), that it copies onto the
child's stack next to argv.  Micronix has none of it - no getenv/putenv in
libc, no `environ` in crt0, no shell variables to export.

It is not a big lift: envp is just another pointer array laid on the stack
with a string payload, exactly like argv, and exec already builds that
stack in putargs().  The pieces are:

  - exec(2) / execve: a third parameter (envp), stacked after argv, plus
    an `environ` pointer (u.environ) and argc/argv/envp aligned the way
    crt0 expects.
  - crt0: set up `environ` from the stack and pass it to main.
  - libc: getenv(3) / putenv(3) (or setenv), and `extern char **environ`.
  - the shell: export/name=value syntax and passing its environment to
    each exec.

--------------------

mw.c stops at 8 heads, and 16 is not a table entry away.  The head
select field in the command structure is three bits:

	hedsel = pcmp<<7 | hicur<<6 | (~head&7)<<2 | drv

so the 8th head is the last one that fits.  The two bits above the head
field are per-track flags the driver sets out of the table, and they are
not the same kind of thing:

	bit 6, hicur - the write current control.  mw.c sets HIGHCUR 0x40
	there and clears it at track >= lowcur, so the driver drops to low
	current partway out.

	bit 7, pcmp - write precompensation.  This is a different function
	and not a current setting at all: it retimes the write data on the
	inner tracks to compensate for bit shift, so it changes the data
	clocking.  It stays.

On the HD-DMA cable bit 6's wire is the same wire as the fourth head
select line, so a 16-head drive overloads it - and only it.

Nothing worth having is lost by giving the pin to head select.  Low
current on the larger MFM drives is generated by the drive itself - it
knows what cylinder it is on and modulates its own write current - and
the line exists for the early drives that had to be told.  Even smaller
later 8-head drives routinely ignore it.  So the wire was on its way
out anyway, and the drives large enough to want 16 heads are the ones
that stopped needing to be told.

That makes specs[]'s lowcur, and the HIGHCUR half of hedsel, things to
remove rather than work around, and it is the removal that has to come
first: hedsel widens to (~head&15)<<2, bit 6 goes with it, and the
precompensation at bit 7 does not move.  Any drive that does still want
its current commanded needs the decision made before the field widens,
not after.

None of the types are in the way - struct info.heads is a UINT8 and 16
fits - and neither is specs[]: it is a table, and it loses a column
rather than gaining a row.

